{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`scripts/mutation_gate.py` isolates the **source tree** (`git archive` into a temp dir)\nbut not **runtime effects**. On 2026-08-26 a mutation removed a guard from a shell\nscript that performs real deletion, a test exec'd that script with an empty path\nvariable, and the glob expanded to `rm -rf /*` — destroying `/opt/homebrew` and every\nHomebrew-installed app in `/Applications` on a contributor workstation. Only a\n60-second subprocess timeout stopped it; `/Users` was still ahead of it in the target\nlist.\n\nScope is the **OS sandbox only**. The static lint gate bundled here was split to\n`6818-615f-555e-4bb9` per plan review (G5).\n\n## Which subprocess matters\n\n`execute_shard()` spawns four subprocesses via `_run()`. Only two execute shard test\ncode and are in scope: the baseline `pytest` (579) and `mutmut run --max-children 1`\n(583); `mutmut results` (595) and `mutmut show` (612) are reporting-only. The incident\noccurred under `mutmut run`, which executes the *mutated* code — sandboxing only the\nbaseline would leave the causal path exposed.\n\n## Why source-tree isolation cannot help\n\ncargo-mutants, mutmut, StrykerJS, PIT and mull all isolate the source tree; **none\nisolates runtime effects.** A copied tree does nothing when mutated code calls\n`rm -rf /` — the syscall still lands. cargo-mutants documents the hazard\n(https://mutants.rs/cautions.html) and recommends an isolated environment. And because\nthe harness string-substitutes the artifact under test, any guard **inside** it can be\nmutated away — only an outside layer holds.\n\n## Mechanisms\n\n**macOS — Seatbelt.** `sandbox-exec` is Apple-deprecated, so its enforcement is a\nload-bearing external capability. Measured on this host (macOS 26.5.2) with\n`(allow default)` + `(deny file-write*)` + an allow-list subpath: a write outside the\nallow-list was DENIED and the file absent; a write inside succeeded; `rm -rf\n<protected>/*` (the incident's shape) was DENIED and the target survived. Deprecation\nis a future risk, not a current gap — the fail-closed posture turns a future breakage\ninto an abort, not a silent unsandboxed run.\n\n**Linux — bubblewrap only.** The `unshare --map-root-user --mount` fallback is\n**dropped**: it creates a private mount namespace but does not deny writes without a\nread-only remount, and unprivileged `CLONE_NEWUSER` is disabled on many hardened hosts —\nso it could probe as present yet fail to enforce, which is worse than no sandbox because\nit looks safe. Without `bwrap`, Linux fails closed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Both shard-test-executing subprocesses in `execute_shard()` — the baseline\n      `pytest` (line 579) **and** the `mutmut run` mutant test-run (line 583) — are\n      wrapped in an OS-enforced sandbox denying filesystem writes outside an explicit\n      allow-list (scratch tree, pytest basetemp, the venv, `/dev/null`). Verified by\n      `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`.\n- [x] The enforcement test trips the deny under **`mutmut run`** specifically, not only\n      under the baseline `pytest`, since `mutmut run` is the path the incident took.\n- [x] The sandbox demonstrably DENIES: a probe writing outside the allow-list exits\n      non-zero and the file is absent afterward; the same probe inside the allow-list\n      succeeds. Asserting wrapper argv alone does not satisfy this — the test observes\n      enforcement, not construction.\n- [x] macOS uses Seatbelt `sandbox-exec` with a generated profile, reproducing the\n      three probes measured above. Verified by `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`\n      on a macOS host.\n- [x] Linux uses `bwrap`, probed at runtime. No `unshare` fallback. Verified by a test\n      asserting the probe selects `bwrap` when present and returns \"unavailable\" when\n      absent.\n- [x] The Linux write-deny is proven **on Linux**, not inferred from the macOS result:\n      the three probes were measured by hand in a `ubuntu:24.04` container — write\n      outside the allow-list DENIED, write inside succeeded, `rm -rf <protected>/*`\n      DENIED with the target surviving. It is NOT proven by a green `ubuntu-latest` CI\n      leg: per the criterion below, those runners deny unprivileged user namespaces, so\n      `probe()` returns None and the enforcement tests SKIP — a green leg there proves\n      nothing about `bwrap`. Closing that gap with a gating lane is tracked separately\n      as `0d22-f664-77e1-495f`.\n- [x] The sandbox wrapper preserves the existing `mutmut run --max-children 1`\n      concurrency contract asserted at `tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py:948`.\n- [x] With no sandbox mechanism available the run ABORTS non-zero with a message naming\n      `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED`. Verified by a test stubbing the mechanism\n      absent and asserting that literal appears in stderr.\n- [x] `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED=1` waives the sandbox and logs at WARNING; a\n      silent unsandboxed fallback is rejected. Verified by asserting the WARNING record\n      names the same literal.\n- [x] Enforcement is an OS facility invoked in-process, never a CI-provider feature, and\n      works from a developer laptop with no CI involved (`project.portability`).\n- [x] `HOME` is set to a non-existent path for both sandboxed subprocesses so adjacent\n      home-directory writes fail loudly. Verified by asserting the child's `HOME`.\n- [x] `probe()` verifies `bwrap` FUNCTIONALLY, not by `shutil.which`. Presence is not\n      capability: this repo's own CI runners ship bubblewrap but deny unprivileged user\n      namespaces (`setting up uid map: Permission denied`). A PATH-only probe reports\n      available and then fails to enforce — the exact flaw that disqualified `unshare`.\n      Verified by `test_bwrap_present_but_unable_to_namespace_is_not_offered`.\n- [x] On a CI runner (`CI` truthy) with no mechanism, the gate proceeds UNSANDBOXED with\n      a WARNING; on a workstation it still ABORTS. A runner is already the disposable\n      environment the sandbox substitutes for. Keyed on the cross-provider `CI`\n      convention, naming no provider (`project.portability`). Verified by\n      `test_ci_runner_proceeds_unsandboxed_with_a_warning` and\n      `test_workstation_still_aborts_when_no_sandbox`.\n- [x] A usable sandbox is used even on CI — `CI` is a fallback for the ABSENCE of a\n      mechanism, never a licence to skip a working one. Verified by\n      `test_a_working_sandbox_is_used_even_on_ci`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py` — enforcement under both the baseline\n  and `mutmut run`, mechanism probing, fail-closed abort, opt-out WARNING, and `HOME`.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py` — the existing suite still passes,\n  including the `--max-children 1` concurrency contract at line 948 the wrapper must\n  preserve.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`,\n  `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` — all green.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Every acceptance criterion above is checked by the command it names.\n- The sandbox logic lands in its own module: `mutation_gate.py` is **762 LOC against\n  the 800 hard cap**, so it cannot absorb this inline.\n- Operator note documents `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED` and the risk of setting it.\n\n## Platform scope\n\nPOSIX only. `pyproject.toml:37-38` classifies the project `Operating System :: POSIX`\nand `MacOS :: MacOS X`; the CI matrix is `[ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]`. Windows is\nout of scope as an unsupported platform.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled from the 2026-08-26 incident. Two other leaks found the same day —\ngate-snapshot accumulation (`a37c-d55c-72c3-439b`) and orphaned CPU load generators\n(`f3d9-ecd8-df72-4fa1`) — are unrelated in cause and tracked separately. Prior\nplan-review rounds caught four real defects, all corrected above: the `mutmut run`\nscoping (G6), the unnamed opt-out variable (E2), the unverified `unshare` write-deny\nclaim (T1/T3), and AC6's false claim that a green `ubuntu-latest` leg proves Linux\nenforcement."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787856087584822001,"uuid":"8b254e11-5c73-4b56-aa2d-0fc79441c62b"}